#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
plan-tasks-resolve — CLI trampoline over claude-klabauter's plan.tasks.mutate 'resolve'
verb, exposing the five-exit disposition model on PATH for the first time.

Purpose: plan.tasks.mutate's resolve verb (coordinator_core/ops/
plan_tasks_mutate.py) already implements the exit model the PM asked for, but
has no main(), no argparse, and no coordinator/bin/ trampoline — the only
invocation seam was a hand-composed JSON-RPC blob. Across 643 plan-tasks rows
in 74 plan files, that unreachability produced 0 backlogged and 0 wont_do
dispositions. This trampoline is the fix: it holds NO domain logic of its
own (mirrors coordinator/bin/handoff-carry-gate's "the trampoline resolves
CLAUDE_KLABAUTER_ROOT/repo_root and calls into coordinator_core" shape) — every gate
(pm_approved, grouping-approval digest, disposition_detail requirement, D5
ordering, disposition_ref shape) is enforced by the op itself.

No shebang line (POSIX-EXEC-ASSUMPTION-GUARD, 2026-07-28 PM ruling) — this is
a bare extensionless polyglot entrypoint, invoked via its own
coordinator/bin/plan-tasks-resolve.cmd Windows launcher (which resolves a
Python interpreter and runs this file directly) or via an explicit
python coordinator/bin/plan-tasks-resolve ... call on POSIX. Modeled on
coordinator/bin/handoff-carry-gate's "trampoline resolves CLAUDE_KLABAUTER_ROOT/
repo_root and calls into coordinator_core" shape.

The five exits after the 2026-08-05 DoE ruling
(cross-repo/inbox/2026-08-05-doe-claude-em-plan-tasks-five-exits-ruling.md;
docs/plans/2026-08-05-make-the-five-exit-plan-tasks-resolver-r.md):

  --coded <sha>            -> disposition: coded      (no PM word needed)
  --spun-off <ref>         -> disposition: spun_off    (no PM word needed —
                               DoE relaxed the pm_approved gate at bd0475fd5,
                               schema 1.4.0: moving work drops no scope)
  --moved-to <plan-path>   -> disposition: spun_off with an EXISTING target
                               plan (no PM word needed — same relaxation;
                               DoE's ruling: "spun_off with a pre-existing
                               target", not a sixth disposition value)
  --backlogged             -> disposition: backlogged  (PM WORD REQUIRED —
                               retained by PM ruling: "I want to be able to
                               weigh in on backlogged vs won't do.")
  --wont-do                -> disposition: wont_do     (PM WORD REQUIRED —
                               same PM ruling)

Every row takes --id and --plan. --id is repeatable (--id C1 --id C2 ...)
for --coded ONLY (2026-08-06, cross-repo/inbox/2026-08-06-doe-claude-em-
deferrals-both-sides-landed-revendor-and-a-resolve-ergonomics-bug.md), so a
full-plan close-out batch resolves in one atomic op call instead of N
independent partial writes — "these N rows are implemented" is genuinely one
shared statement. Every other exit's --disposition-detail/--case-against (or
--spun-off/--moved-to's --disposition-ref) is PER-ROW rationale, and sharing
one such string across ids would silently launder distinct decisions into
one, so a multi-id call against any other exit is refused locally, naming
the alternative (one call per row). --disposition-detail is REQUIRED for all
five exits, --coded included (D2/D3/D4: the rationale — for --coded this is
evidence of work done rather than a scope decision, but every row leaving
'open' still has to say why, never a synthesised placeholder) — enforced
here at the CLI layer even though the underlying resolve verb's own
enforcement of the wont_do leg may still be mid-landing on a peer chunk as
this ships.

--moved-to is spun_off with an existing target (DoE's ruling; no schema
change) — it is NOT a sixth disposition. Workflow:
  1. Read the SOURCE row (plain local file read, no lock — this repo's own
     plan.tasks.mutate verb has no "read one row" surface, so this mirrors
     plan-tasks-grouping-digest.py's "plain local read, direct import, no
     JSON-RPC round-trip" precedent for a read-only lookup).
  2. add-task the row's content (its disposition fields stripped — the row
     starts OPEN on the target plan) onto the target plan via
     plan.tasks.mutate.
  3. Resolve the SOURCE row to spun_off, disposition_ref = the target
     plan's own path (a single repo-relative path, matching every other
     spun_off ref's shape — _dispatch_spun_off in plan_tasks_mutate.py
     verifies it resolves to a real file, which the target plan already is).

Step 2's add-task response is parsed for the id it ACTUALLY wrote (never
the caller-proposed id trusted blindly) — mirrors _dispatch_backlogged
computing its own disposition_ref from the harvest dispatch's real result
rather than a caller's prediction (DoE's ruling explicitly names this
pattern). add-task does not mint ids today (duplicate id -> abort, no
silent rename), so this is defensive-forward, not dead code: if a future
add-task legitimately mints a non-colliding id, this trampoline already
detects the divergence and folds it into disposition_detail rather than
silently recording a fact ("moved to <plan>") that would then be wrong about
which row it moved to.

The two PM-gated exits refuse with a message naming what the EM should ask
the PM for (op-side _LEGACY_PM_APPROVAL_HINT/_GROUPING_APPROVAL_HINT,
surfaced verbatim through RouteMutationError) — this trampoline does not
invent its own refusal text, and carries NO bypass flag, env override, or
--force. That gate is deliberate and retained by PM ruling.

Usage:
  plan-tasks-resolve --id <row-id> --plan <plan-path> <ONE exit flag> \\
      --disposition-detail "<why>"

Exit codes:
  0 — resolve (and, for --moved-to, the preceding add-task) applied.
  1 — op-level refusal (MutateAbort: unknown id, PM gate ungated, missing
      disposition_detail, D5 ordering violation, bad disposition_ref, ...) or
      plan/row not found.
  2 — usage error (bad args, more than one exit flag, missing
      --disposition-detail on a gated exit) or transport failure (CLAUDE_KLABAUTER_ROOT
      unresolvable, coordinator_core.invoke seam absent/broken).

Spec backlink: coordinator_core/ops/plan_tasks_mutate.py (resolve verb)
Spec backlink: pln-make-the-five-exit-plan-tasks-305b16 § C5
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import ast
import os
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path

_BIN_DIR = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
_LIB_DIR = os.path.join(_BIN_DIR, "lib")
if _LIB_DIR not in sys.path:
    sys.path.insert(0, _LIB_DIR)

import cc_invoke  # noqa: E402
from cc_invoke import require_engine_on_path  # noqa: E402

# The engine root must be on sys.path before `_resolve_repo_root` and
# `_read_source_row` import `coordinator_core` (win_portability,
# frontmatter.body_blocks): this file is also published into the
# claude-klabauter mirror, where coordinator_core is NOT pip-installed and
# the interpreter's sys.path[0] is this bin/ directory, not the checkout
# root. Same bootstrap as coordinator/bin/coordinator-lesson-add (9b979ee5f).
_ENGINE_ROOT = require_engine_on_path(__file__)

_OP = "plan.tasks.mutate"

# Dispositions that require a non-empty --disposition-detail at the CLI layer.
# EVERY non-open disposition needs one, `coded` included. plan_tasks_mutate.py's
# `_PLAN_TASKS_DETAIL_REQUIRED_DISPOSITIONS` covers only the three closed exits
# (D2/D4), but the shared disposition-shape check refuses any non-open row with
# an empty detail — "Every disposition other than 'open' requires
# disposition_detail naming the rationale." Mirroring the narrower constant here
# let `--coded` past the CLI and into a server-side refusal, so this set is
# deliberately WIDER than the one it used to mirror.
_DETAIL_REQUIRED_DISPOSITIONS = frozenset(
    {"coded", "spun_off", "backlogged", "wont_do"}
)

# The two exits the PM retained at the 2026-08-05 ruling — they are where work
# actually gets dropped, and choosing between them is the whole decision.
# Mirrors schema_validate.py's `_PLAN_TASKS_PM_APPROVAL_GATED_DISPOSITIONS`.
# Used only to word refusals accurately; the gate itself is enforced server-side.
_PM_GATED_DISPOSITIONS = frozenset({"backlogged", "wont_do"})

# Dispositions that require a non-empty --case-against at the CLI layer
# (leg 1, 2026-08-06, plan docs/plans/2026-08-06-deferrals-carry-both-sides.md)
# — the SAME two scope-cut exits as _PM_GATED_DISPOSITIONS. Where
# --disposition-detail carries the case FOR this exit, --case-against
# carries the strongest honest case for doing the work now, so the PM sees
# both sides rather than an ID list the EM has already convinced itself of.
# The underlying resolve verb's own `_PLAN_TASKS_CASE_AGAINST_REQUIRED_
# DISPOSITIONS` is the real enforcement; this mirrors it here only to fail
# fast with a usage error instead of a round-trip refusal.
_CASE_AGAINST_REQUIRED_DISPOSITIONS = frozenset({"backlogged", "wont_do"})

# The offer-shaped refusal for a multi-id call against any exit other than
# --coded (leg 2, cross-repo/inbox/2026-08-06-doe-claude-em-deferrals-both-
# sides-landed-revendor-and-a-resolve-ergonomics-bug.md). --coded is the one
# exit where "these N rows are implemented" is genuinely a single shared
# statement — every other exit's --disposition-detail/--case-against (or, for
# --spun-off/--moved-to, --disposition_ref) is PER-ROW rationale, and a
# repeatable --id sharing one such string would silently launder N distinct
# decisions into one. Named here, not just enforced, per the dispatch brief's
# "name WHY, and name the alternative" instruction.
_MULTI_ID_ONLY_FOR_CODED_MESSAGE = (
    "repeatable --id is only offered for --coded — {disposition!r} needs "
    "PER-ROW rationale (each row's case for and against is the whole point "
    "of this disposition), and a shared --disposition-detail/--case-against "
    "across multiple ids would silently launder distinct decisions into one. "
    "Make one call per row instead."
)

_EPILOG = """\
Repeatable --id (--id C1 --id C2 ...) is accepted for --coded ONLY — "these
rows are implemented" is genuinely one shared statement, sent as a single
atomic batch. Every other exit needs PER-ROW rationale and refuses more than
one --id; make one call per row instead.

The five exits (2026-08-05 DoE ruling):

  --coded <sha>            -> disposition: coded      (no PM word needed)
  --spun-off <ref>         -> disposition: spun_off    (no PM word needed)
  --moved-to <plan-path>   -> disposition: spun_off with an existing target
                               (no PM word needed)
  --backlogged             -> disposition: backlogged  (PM WORD REQUIRED)
  --wont-do                -> disposition: wont_do     (PM WORD REQUIRED)

--disposition-detail is REQUIRED for every exit, --coded included: the
rationale, not a synthesised placeholder. Any row leaving 'open' has to say
why in prose — the SHA records what shipped, the detail records what it was.

--case-against is REQUIRED for --backlogged and --wont-do (the two PM-word
exits, and the only two that actually cut scope — nothing leaves the corpus
on --spun-off/--moved-to, so there is no cut to argue against): the
strongest honest case for doing the work now, not a strawman. Where
--disposition-detail carries the case FOR this exit, --case-against carries
the case AGAINST, so the PM is shown a real decision, not an ID list the EM
has already convinced itself of.

Examples:
  plan-tasks-resolve --id C7 --plan docs/plans/foo.md --coded a1b2c3d
  plan-tasks-resolve --id C7 --plan docs/plans/foo.md --spun-off \\
      docs/spinoffs/2026-08-05-foo.md --disposition-detail "split out"
  plan-tasks-resolve --id C7 --plan docs/plans/foo.md \\
      --moved-to docs/plans/bar.md --disposition-detail "folded into bar"
  plan-tasks-resolve --id C7 --plan docs/plans/foo.md --backlogged \\
      --disposition-detail "PM: defer to next quarter" \\
      --case-against "nothing blocks on it, but it fixes a real papercut"
  plan-tasks-resolve --id C7 --plan docs/plans/foo.md --wont-do \\
      --disposition-detail "PM: superseded by bar" \\
      --case-against "closest thing to a safety net if bar regresses"
"""


def _resolve_repo_root() -> str:
    """Resolve the CALLER's repo root — mirrors every sibling trampoline's
    shape (query-records.py/lib/records_query.py, append-plan-session.py,
    prune-closed-bugs.py, sweep-boot.py, emit-cockpit-snapshot.py): `git
    rev-parse --show-toplevel` from cwd, falling back to os.getcwd() on any
    git failure. This is the CALLER's own repo (route_mutation's third
    positional), never claude-klabauter's own checkout.
    """
    try:
        from coordinator_core.git.repo_root import show_toplevel

        resolved = show_toplevel()
        if resolved:
            return resolved
    except OSError:
        pass
    return os.getcwd()


def _no_legacy() -> None:
    """State-1 (seam-absent) handler — claude-klabauter is a mandatory
    coordinator dependency; there is no legacy body to fall back to."""
    raise RuntimeError(
        "plan-tasks-resolve: coordinator_core.invoke seam absent — no legacy "
        "path exists for plan.tasks.mutate. Verify CLAUDE_KLABAUTER_ROOT and the "
        "claude-klabauter coordinator_core install."
    )


def _build_parser() -> argparse.ArgumentParser:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="plan-tasks-resolve",
        description=(
            "Resolve a plan '## Tasks' spine row to one of five terminal "
            "exits via plan.tasks.mutate's resolve verb."
        ),
        epilog=_EPILOG,
        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter,
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--id",
        action="append",
        dest="task_ids",
        default=[],
        help=(
            "the task row's id — repeatable (--id C1 --id C2 ...) for "
            "--coded ONLY, to resolve a whole batch in one atomic call; "
            "every other exit requires per-row rationale and rejects "
            "more than one --id"
        ),
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--plan", required=True, dest="plan_path", help="path to the plan file (docs/plans/...)"
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--disposition-detail",
        dest="disposition_detail",
        default=None,
        help=(
            "the rationale for this exit — REQUIRED for all five, "
            "--coded included"
        ),
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--case-against",
        dest="case_against",
        default=None,
        help=(
            "the strongest honest case for doing the work now — REQUIRED "
            "for --backlogged/--wont-do only (the two scope-cut exits); "
            "the both-sides counterpart to --disposition-detail"
        ),
    )

    exits = parser.add_mutually_exclusive_group(required=True)
    exits.add_argument("--coded", dest="coded", metavar="SHA", help="disposition: coded")
    exits.add_argument("--spun-off", dest="spun_off", metavar="REF", help="disposition: spun_off")
    exits.add_argument(
        "--moved-to",
        dest="moved_to",
        metavar="PLAN_PATH",
        help="disposition: spun_off, with an existing target plan",
    )
    exits.add_argument(
        "--backlogged", dest="backlogged", action="store_true", help="disposition: backlogged"
    )
    exits.add_argument(
        "--wont-do", dest="wont_do", action="store_true", help="disposition: wont_do"
    )
    return parser


def _selected_disposition(args: argparse.Namespace) -> tuple[str, str | None]:
    """Return (disposition, disposition_ref) for whichever exit flag was given.

    The mutually exclusive group (required=True) already guarantees exactly
    one of the five was supplied.
    """
    if args.coded is not None:
        return "coded", args.coded
    if args.spun_off is not None:
        return "spun_off", args.spun_off
    if args.moved_to is not None:
        return "spun_off", None  # disposition_ref computed by _do_moved_to
    if args.backlogged:
        return "backlogged", None
    return "wont_do", None


def _parse_written_id(message: str, fallback: str) -> str:
    """Extract the id add-task's own response reports it actually wrote.

    `_add_task`'s success message ends `added task {task['id']!r}` (Python
    repr, single- or double-quoted depending on the id's own content) — parsed
    via `ast.literal_eval` on the trailing token rather than a bare strip of
    quote characters, so an id containing an apostrophe round-trips correctly.
    Falls back to `fallback` (the id this trampoline proposed) if the message
    does not match the expected shape — add-task's own response is still the
    source of truth on the happy path this defends; failing back to the
    caller's own proposal is a diagnostic degrade, not a silent success claim.
    """
    marker = "added task "
    idx = message.rfind(marker)
    if idx == -1:
        return fallback
    token = message[idx + len(marker):].strip()
    try:
        parsed = ast.literal_eval(token)
    except (ValueError, SyntaxError):
        return fallback
    return parsed if isinstance(parsed, str) and parsed else fallback


def _read_source_row(plan_path: str, task_id: str, repo_root: str) -> dict:
    """Read the SOURCE row's current fields via a plain local file read.

    Read-only lookup — no lock is taken, no write occurs. Mirrors
    plan-tasks-grouping-digest.py's own "plain local read, direct import, no
    JSON-RPC round-trip" precedent: `plan.tasks.mutate` has no "read one row"
    verb, so a --moved-to add-task call needs the row's content from disk
    directly, in-process, via the same locator the mutate op itself uses
    (coordinator_core.frontmatter.body_blocks.locate_fenced_block) — never a
    second hand-rolled fence-locate implementation.

    Raises RuntimeError (never returns a partial/None) on any resolution
    failure: CLAUDE_KLABAUTER_ROOT unresolvable, plan not found, spine malformed/
    absent, or the id not present in the spine.
    """
    # Review: coordinator:code-reviewer — fail-loud site must stay fail-loud;
    # ensure_engine_on_path degrades to None and would surface a bare
    # ImportError instead of this function's documented RuntimeError contract.
    cc_invoke.require_engine_on_path(__file__)
    import yaml
    from coordinator_core.frontmatter.body_blocks import LocateStatus, locate_fenced_block

    p = Path(plan_path)
    if not p.is_absolute():
        p = Path(repo_root) / p
    try:
        text = p.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError as exc:
        raise RuntimeError(f"plan-tasks-resolve: cannot read plan {plan_path!r}: {exc}") from exc

    result = locate_fenced_block(text)
    if result.status is not LocateStatus.LOCATED:
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"plan-tasks-resolve: task spine in {plan_path!r} is absent or malformed "
            "— cannot read source row for --moved-to"
        )
    rows = yaml.safe_load(result.body) or []
    if not isinstance(rows, list):
        raise RuntimeError(f"plan-tasks-resolve: task spine body in {plan_path!r} is not a list")
    for row in rows:
        if isinstance(row, dict) and row.get("id") == task_id:
            return dict(row)
    raise RuntimeError(f"plan-tasks-resolve: task id {task_id!r} not found in {plan_path!r}")


# Fields resolve's write path owns exclusively (D4) — stripped from the row
# copied onto the target plan so the moved row starts OPEN there, mirroring
# `plan_tasks_mutate._STAMP_RESERVED_DISPOSITION_FIELDS`.
_RESERVED_DISPOSITION_FIELDS = frozenset({"disposition", "disposition_ref", "disposition_detail"})


def _do_moved_to(
    task_id: str,
    plan_path: str,
    moved_to_path: str,
    disposition_detail: str,
    repo_root: str,
) -> dict:
    """Apply the --moved-to exit: add-task onto the target plan, then resolve
    the source row to spun_off with the target plan itself as disposition_ref.

    See module docstring's --moved-to section for the full rationale.
    """
    source_row = _read_source_row(plan_path, task_id, repo_root)
    task = {k: v for k, v in source_row.items() if k not in _RESERVED_DISPOSITION_FIELDS}
    proposed_id = task.get("id", task_id)

    add_result = cc_invoke.route_mutation(
        _OP,
        {"verb": "add-task", "plan_path": moved_to_path, "task": task},
        repo_root,
        _no_legacy,
    )
    message = add_result.get("message", "") if isinstance(add_result, dict) else ""
    written_id = _parse_written_id(message, proposed_id)

    final_detail = disposition_detail
    if written_id != proposed_id:
        final_detail = (
            f"{disposition_detail} (moved to {moved_to_path!r} as task "
            f"{written_id!r}, not the proposed id {proposed_id!r})"
        )

    try:
        return cc_invoke.route_mutation(
            _OP,
            {
                "verb": "resolve",
                "plan_path": plan_path,
                "id": task_id,
                "disposition": "spun_off",
                "disposition_ref": moved_to_path,
                "disposition_detail": final_detail,
            },
            repo_root,
            _no_legacy,
        )
    except cc_invoke.RouteMutationError as exc:
        # Review: code-reviewer (Finding 1) — add-task onto the target plan
        # already applied by this point; a resolve failure here is a PARTIAL
        # mutation, not a plain refusal. Re-raise with the target plan/id
        # named so the operator isn't left to discover the duplicate row on
        # a naive retry via a bare "add-task: duplicate task id" error.
        raise cc_invoke.RouteMutationError(
            f"add-task to {moved_to_path!r} already applied (id {written_id!r}); "
            f"resolve on the source row {task_id!r} in {plan_path!r} failed: {exc}",
            exc.result,
        ) from exc


def _exit_code_from_route_mutation_error(exc: cc_invoke.RouteMutationError) -> int:
    """Coerce a RouteMutationError's carried exit_code to a nonzero int.

    Mirrors append-plan-session.py's own coercion: the op's `_err` shape
    always carries `exit_code: 1` today, but this stays defensive against a
    future differently-coded refusal rather than hardcoding 1.
    """
    result = exc.result if isinstance(exc.result, dict) else {}
    raw = result.get("exit_code")
    try:
        code = int(raw) if raw is not None else 1
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        code = 1
    return code if code != 0 else 1


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    parser = _build_parser()
    args = parser.parse_args(argv if argv is not None else sys.argv[1:])

    disposition, disposition_ref = _selected_disposition(args)

    if not args.task_ids:
        parser.error("--id is required")

    if len(args.task_ids) > 1 and disposition != "coded":
        parser.error(_MULTI_ID_ONLY_FOR_CODED_MESSAGE.format(disposition=disposition))

    if disposition in _DETAIL_REQUIRED_DISPOSITIONS and not (
        args.disposition_detail and args.disposition_detail.strip()
    ):
        whose = "the PM's reasoning" if disposition in _PM_GATED_DISPOSITIONS else "the rationale"
        parser.error(
            f"--disposition-detail is required for disposition {disposition!r} "
            f"({whose} — every row leaving 'open' has to say why)"
        )

    if disposition in _CASE_AGAINST_REQUIRED_DISPOSITIONS and not (
        args.case_against and args.case_against.strip()
    ):
        parser.error(
            f"--case-against is required for disposition {disposition!r} "
            "(the strongest honest case for doing the work now — this is a "
            "scope cut, and the PM needs both sides, not just the case for it)"
        )

    repo_root = _resolve_repo_root()

    try:
        if args.moved_to is not None:
            result = _do_moved_to(
                args.task_ids[0],
                args.plan_path,
                args.moved_to,
                args.disposition_detail or "",
                repo_root,
            )
        elif len(args.task_ids) > 1:
            # Multi-id --coded batch: one atomic call carrying every named
            # row's resolution, via the op's existing `resolves` batch shape
            # (C13, 2026-07-30) — no new op-side surface needed.
            resolves = []
            for task_id in args.task_ids:
                entry: dict[str, object] = {
                    "id": task_id,
                    "disposition": disposition,
                }
                if disposition_ref is not None:
                    entry["disposition_ref"] = disposition_ref
                if args.disposition_detail:
                    entry["disposition_detail"] = args.disposition_detail
                if args.case_against:
                    entry["case_against"] = args.case_against
                resolves.append(entry)
            params = {
                "verb": "resolve",
                "plan_path": args.plan_path,
                "resolves": resolves,
            }
            result = cc_invoke.route_mutation(_OP, params, repo_root, _no_legacy)
        else:
            params: dict[str, object] = {
                "verb": "resolve",
                "plan_path": args.plan_path,
                "id": args.task_ids[0],
                "disposition": disposition,
            }
            if disposition_ref is not None:
                params["disposition_ref"] = disposition_ref
            if args.disposition_detail:
                params["disposition_detail"] = args.disposition_detail
            if args.case_against:
                params["case_against"] = args.case_against
            result = cc_invoke.route_mutation(_OP, params, repo_root, _no_legacy)
    except cc_invoke.RouteMutationError as exc:
        print(f"plan-tasks-resolve: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return _exit_code_from_route_mutation_error(exc)
    except RuntimeError as exc:
        # Review: code-reviewer (Finding 1) — moved the fail-safe-state
        # reasoning off this branch: a RuntimeError here (transport failure,
        # State-3; seam-absent, State-1; or _read_source_row's --moved-to
        # lookup failing before any mutation is attempted) means NO write
        # happened at all — there is no partial state to describe. The
        # add-task-applied/resolve-failed partial-mutation case is a
        # RouteMutationError, not a RuntimeError, and is now handled and
        # reported explicitly inside _do_moved_to itself.
        print(f"plan-tasks-resolve: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    message = result.get("message", "") if isinstance(result, dict) else ""
    if message:
        print(message)
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
